              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Calcium is a vital mineral that your body needs to build and maintain strong bones, support heart and muscle function, and transmit nerve signals. \n\nMost adults need about **1,000 mg of calcium per day**, though women over 50 and men over 70 should aim for **1,200 mg per day**. \n\nHere is a comprehensive list of calcium-rich foods categorized by type, complete with approximate calcium amounts per serving.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Dairy Products\nDairy is one of the most well-known and easily absorbed (bioavailable) sources of calcium.\n* **Ricotta Cheese (Part-skim):** ~335 mg per \u00bd cup\n* **Plain Yogurt (Low-fat):** ~310 mg per 6 oz\n* **Cow's Milk (Whole, low-fat, or skim):** ~300 mg per 1 cup (8 oz)\n* **Greek Yogurt:** ~200 mg per 6 oz\n* **Mozzarella Cheese:** ~222 mg per 1 oz\n* **Cheddar Cheese:** ~205 mg per 1 oz\n\n### 2. Plant-Based & Vegan Sources\nIf you do not eat dairy, there are plenty of excellent, plant-based ways to meet your daily calcium requirements.\n* **Tofu (Prepared with calcium sulfate):** ~434 mg per \u00bd cup *(Be sure to check the ingredient label for \"calcium sulfate\")*.\n* **Collard Greens (Cooked):** ~266 mg per 1 cup\n* **Chia Seeds:** ~177 mg per 1 oz (approx. 2 tablespoons)\n* **Kale (Cooked):** ~179 mg per 1 cup\n* **Bok Choy / Pak Choi (Cooked):** ~160 mg per 1 cup\n* **White Beans (Cooked):** ~132 mg per 1 cup\n* **Almonds:** ~75 mg per 1 oz (about 23 whole almonds)\n\n### 3. Seafood (with Bones)\nCanned fish that contains soft, edible bones is exceptionally high in calcium.\n* **Sardines (Canned in oil, with bones):** ~325 mg per 3 oz\n* **Salmon (Canned, with bones):** ~180 mg per 3 oz\n\n### 4. Fortified Foods & Beverages\nMany products are intentionally fortified with calcium to help people meet their nutritional goals.\n* **Fortified Plant Milks (Soy, Almond, Oat):** ~300 to 450 mg per 1 cup *(Varies by brand; check the label)*.\n* **Fortified Orange Juice:** ~300 to 350 mg per 1 cup\n* **Fortified Breakfast Cereals:** ~100 to 1,000 mg per serving *(Check nutrition labels, as fortification levels vary wildly)*.\n\n---\n\n### Tips for Maximizing Calcium Absorption\n* **Pair with Vitamin D:** Your body cannot properly absorb calcium without adequate Vitamin D. Ensure you get enough Vitamin D through sunlight exposure, egg yolks, fatty fish, or fortified foods/supplements.\n* **Watch out for Oxalates:** Some healthy greens like spinach, beet greens, and Swiss chard are high in calcium but also high in compounds called **oxalates**. Oxalates bind to calcium, preventing your body from absorbing most of it. Opt for low-oxalate greens like kale, bok choy, and broccoli for highly absorbable calcium.\n* **Spread it out:** Your body absorbs calcium most efficiently in doses of **500 mg or less** at one time. If you are aiming for 1,000 mg a day, try to get some at breakfast and some at dinner, rather than consuming it all in one heavy meal.",
